<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Van</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Van is the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, a salty soda lake on the high plateau of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Its shores were the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> kingdom of Urartu, whose capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushpa">Tushpa</a> stood at Van, and later of the Armenian kingdom of Vaspurakan. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> Kurdish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> dynasties such as the Shah-Armens and the Qara Qoyunlu held its towns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-van.jpg" alt="Lake Van" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:STS079-781-53.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Van lies in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, in the provinces of Van and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitlis">Bitlis</a>, in the Armenian highlands, at 1,640 metres above sea level. It covers 3,755 square kilometres and is 119 kilometres across at its widest. Its average depth is 171 metres. The water is strongly alkaline and rich in sodium carbonate, and its salinity usually keeps it from freezing. The lake has no outlet. A volcanic eruption blocked its original outlet in prehistoric times, and the lake is primarily tectonic, formed more than 600,000 years ago by the subsidence of a block of crust along faults. The stratovolcano Mount Süphan stands on the northern shore, and the dormant volcano <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut">Mount Nemrut</a> close to the western tip.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The lake was the centre of the kingdom of Urartu from about 1000 BCE, afterwards of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satrapy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, the Kingdom of Greater Armenia and the Armenian Kingdom of Vaspurakan. Tushpa, the capital of Urartu, stood near the shore, on the site of what became the castle of medieval Van. Assyrian sources called the lake the Upper Sea of Nairi, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a> the Lower Sea. Greek-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> called it Thospitis limne.</p><p>In the 11th century the lake lay on the border between the Eastern Roman Empire and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>. Emperor Romanus IV Diogenes was defeated and captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a>, north of the lake, on 26 August 1071. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> divided the conquered lands among his Turcoman generals, and gave the region around the lake to Sökmen el-Kutbî, who made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahlat">Ahlat</a> his capital. The Shah-Armens, also called Sökmenler, ruled the area from 1085 to 1192 and were succeeded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a>. The Ilkhanate Mongols later took the region, and it passed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and Safavid Iran until Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> took control for good.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lake is the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Its shores are surrounded by fruit orchards and grain fields, and the region is known for the Van cat breed. On Akdamar Island stands the 10th-century Cathedral of the Holy Cross, the royal church of the kingdom of Vaspurakan, and the other three main islands also have Armenian religious buildings. The Ahlatshahs left a large number of headstones near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahlat">Ahlat</a>, tentatively listed for World Heritage status. A train ferry crosses the lake between Tatvan and Van. According to legend a monster lives in it, but there is no evidence of one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Van” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Urartu, which its own kings called Biainili, took shape in the ninth century BCE in the highlands around <b>Lake Van</b> in eastern Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling family descended from the Baharlu of the Yıwa tribe of the Oghuz Turks, who by the fourteenth century held lands north of <b>Lake Van</b> and around Mosul in Upper Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Known mainly from brief mentions in Arabic chronicles, the dynasty descended from a Turkish commander named Dilmac who received Bitlis and Erzen, south-west of <b>Lake Van</b>, after the Seljuk conquest of the region in the late 11th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/dilmacogullari">Dilmaçoğulları</a></li><li><blockquote>Bitlis, at a gorge south-west of <b>Lake Van</b>, came under the Rojiki, a Kurdish tribal confederation, whose emirs claimed descent from ancient Kurdish lords.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-bitlis">Emirate of Bitlis</a></li><li><blockquote>The Artsruni family had long held the lands south and east of <b>Lake Van</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vaspurakan">Kingdom of Vaspurakan</a></li><li><blockquote>Nairi was the Assyrian name for the mountain lands north of the Tigris around <b>Lake Van</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nairi">Nairi</a></li></ul></section>
